WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.856
Output length is the main driver.

2
00:00:02.136 --> 00:00:07.064
Because output tokens are generated one at a time, their count usually dominates.

3
00:00:07.344 --> 00:00:20.912
OpenAI’s latency guide puts the asymmetry in numbers: cutting output tokens by half may cut latency by about half, while cutting input tokens by half may improve it by only 1 to 5 percent.

4
00:00:21.192 --> 00:00:33.245
Anthropic’s latency cookbook measured the input side directly on its sample agent: about a quarter of a second of TTFT per 100,000 extra input tokens.

5
00:00:33.525 --> 00:00:37.749
The levers on output, roughly from least to most risky: Shape the answer.

6
00:00:38.029 --> 00:00:46.051
Ask for a specific format with an example (“two lines: decision, then reason”), or return structured fields instead of prose.

7
00:00:46.331 --> 00:00:51.664
In the cookbook, asking for a two-line decision format cut one decision from 46.

8
00:00:51.944 --> 00:00:53.373
4 seconds to 4.

9
00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:58.475
9, and its thinking tokens from 2,083 to 296.

10
00:00:58.755 --> 00:01:07.757
Anthropic’s page also notes that asking for a number of sentences or paragraphs works better than a word count, because the model counts in tokens.

11
00:01:08.037 --> 00:01:12.944
Remove commentary between tool calls, where the agent narrates what it is about to do.

12
00:01:13.224 --> 00:01:15.037
Lower effort on routine routes.

13
00:01:15.317 --> 00:01:26.133
In the cookbook, low effort cut median task time by 13 percent with a small accuracy drop; high added 7 percent to the median and 12 percent to the p90.

14
00:01:26.413 --> 00:01:28.568
Turn thinking off where the model allows it.

15
00:01:28.848 --> 00:01:34.949
This is the steepest capability trade; in the cookbook’s Haiku run, accuracy fell from 97.

16
00:01:35.229 --> 00:01:36.637
5 to 73.

17
00:01:36.917 --> 00:01:40.203
5 percent while the median wait fell only from 4.

18
00:01:40.483 --> 00:01:41.485
9 to 3.

19
00:01:41.765 --> 00:01:42.853
7 seconds.

20
00:01:43.133 --> 00:01:48.061
Every one of these changes what the model writes, so each needs your eval before you keep it.

21
00:01:48.341 --> 00:01:53.717
A shorter answer that drops a required caveat is a regression, not an optimization.
